# Human Development and Sustainable Development

> National income tells you the size of an economy; human development
> asks whether its people live longer, learn more, and earn better —
> and sustainable development asks whether today's progress steals
> from tomorrow. This lesson covers both frameworks and India's record.

## 1. Why GDP Is Not Enough

GDP measures the volume of goods and services, not the lives those
goods build. Two countries can share the same per-capita income while
one starves its children and the other schools them. High income
itself is only *instrumental* — valuable for what it enables.

[definition]
**Human development** is the process of enlarging people's choices and
capabilities — especially a **long and healthy life, knowledge, and a
decent standard of living**. The idea was launched by Pakistani
economist **Mahbub ul Haq** with Indian Nobel laureate **Amartya Sen**
(capabilities approach); the first **Human Development Report (HDR)**
appeared in **1990** with its famous opening: "People are the real
wealth of a nation."
[/definition]

The shift matters for policy: if development is capability, then
public spending on health, education, and sanitation is not "welfare
leakage" but core investment — a direct challenge to treating
schools as a residual after factories.

## 2. The Human Development Index (HDI)

The UNDP's HDI compresses three dimensions into one number between 0
and 1, using the **geometric mean** of three sub-indices (the method
since 2010):

@diagram tree id="hdi-structure" layout="tree" aria="Dimensions and indicators of the Human Development Index"
  HDI -> "Health: life expectancy at birth"
  HDI -> "Education: expected and mean years of schooling"
  HDI -> "Standard of living: GNI per capita (PPP)"
  "Education: expected and mean years of schooling" -> "Expected: 0-18 years"
  "Education: expected and mean years of schooling" -> "Mean: 0-15 years"
  "Standard of living: GNI per capita (PPP)" -> "Log scale, US$100-75,000"
@/diagram

| Dimension | Indicator | Goalposts |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Health | Life expectancy at birth | 20 to 85 years |
| Education | Expected years of schooling | 0 to 18 years |
| Education | Mean years of schooling | 0 to 15 years |
| Living standard | GNI per capita (PPP $) | 100 to 75,000 (logarithm) |

Income enters on a **logarithm** so that an extra dollar matters most
to the poorest — the HDI deliberately tapers the weight of riches.
Values are banded: below 0.550 is **low**, 0.550-0.699 **medium**,
0.700-0.799 **high**, and 0.800+ **very high** human development.

India's trajectory, reading HDR values (approximate):

@chart line title="India's HDI value (about), selected years" labels:
1990
2000
2010
2022

series:
  - name: "HDI"
    data: 0.43 0.50 0.59 0.64
@/chart

India sits in the **medium** band — about 0.64, ranked in the low
130s among 193 countries in recent Reports (Sri Lanka is high-band;
Nepal and Bangladesh trail close behind, with Bangladesh closing
fast). Within India, Kerala has always topped state-level HDI
computations, with Bihar at the bottom — a within-country gap larger
than many international ones.

[warning]
A classic error: reading HDI as an average of the whole population's
well-being. HDI uses national averages, so deep inequality can hide
behind a respectable mean. That is exactly why UNDP later introduced
the inequality-adjusted indices below.
[/warning]

## 3. Beyond HDI: A Family of Indices

| Index | What it adds | Note for India |
| --- | --- | --- |
| IHDI | HDI discounted by inequality in each dimension | India's loss is roughly a fifth — large |
| Gender Development Index (GDI) | Female HDI relative to male HDI | Ratio below 1 signals a gender gap |
| Gender Inequality Index (GII) | Maternal mortality, adolescent births, parliamentary seats, schooling | India's GII value is about 0.44 |
| Global MPI (UNDP-OPHI) | Ten indicators of deprivation across health, education, living standards | India: 55.1% poor (2005-06) to 16.4% (2019-21) |

The **Multidimensional Poverty Index** deserves special attention
because it replaces income with *deprivations*: a household is poor
if it is deprived in a weighted third of the ten indicators — say,
malnourishment plus no electricity plus a dirt floor. UNDP estimates
about **415 million Indians exited multidimensional poverty between
2005-06 and 2019-21**, the fastest such decline ever recorded for a
country of this size. NITI Aayog's **National MPI** (twelve
indicators, adding Maternity care and Banking) reports a fall from
24.85 per cent (2015-16) to 14.96 per cent (2019-21).

## 4. India's Human Development Ledger

Basic social indicators behind the HDI numbers:

| Indicator | Approximate value | Source/era |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Life expectancy at birth | About 70 years | SRS, 2016-20 |
| Infant mortality rate | About 28 per 1,000 live births | SRS |
| Literacy rate | 74% (Census 2011); about 78% in later surveys | Census / NSO |
| Mean years of schooling | About 6.5-7 years | HDR |
| GNI per capita | About US$7,000 (PPP) | HDR |

Programmes that moved the needle: the **Right to Education Act
(2009)** and Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan for schooling; **Ayushman Bharat
(2018)** for hospitalisation cover; the **National Food Security Act
(2013)** guaranteeing subsidised grain to two-thirds of the
population; **Jal Jeevan Mission** for tap-water connections; and
**POSHAN Abhiyaan** against child malnutrition. Each maps to an HDI
dimension — health, knowledge, or living standards.

## 5. Sustainable Development: Meaning and Milestones

[definition]
**Sustainable development** is development that **meets the needs of
the present without compromising the ability of future generations to
meet their own needs** — the definition of the 1987 Brundtland
Report, *Our Common Future*.
[/definition]

The concept grew through a chain of milestones, each worth memorising:

| Year | Event | Contribution |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1972 | Stockholm Conference | First UN environment conference; UNEP created |
| 1987 | Brundtland Report | Canonical definition; intergenerational equity |
| 1992 | Rio Earth Summit | Agenda 21; climate and biodiversity conventions |
| 1997 | Kyoto Protocol | Binding emission cuts for developed countries |
| 2000 | Millennium Development Goals | 8 goals for 2000-2015 |
| 2012 | Rio+20 | Mandate to design the SDGs |
| 2015 | 2030 Agenda | SDGs adopted, effective 1 January 2016 |

The MDGs were goals for *developing* countries, largely funded and
prodded by donors; the SDGs are **universal** — 17 goals and 169
targets that apply to Germany and Ghana alike, binding the three
axes of economy, society, and environment.

## 6. The 17 SDGs and the Five Ps

The 2030 Agenda organises the goals under five headings, the **Five
Ps**:

@diagram tree id="sdg-five-ps" layout="tree" aria="The 17 SDGs grouped under the five Ps"
  "2030 Agenda (17 goals, 169 targets)" -> People
  "2030 Agenda (17 goals, 169 targets)" -> Planet
  "2030 Agenda (17 goals, 169 targets)" -> Prosperity
  "2030 Agenda (17 goals, 169 targets)" -> Peace
  "2030 Agenda (17 goals, 169 targets)" -> Partnership
  People -> "SDGs 1-5: poverty, hunger, health, education, gender"
  Planet -> "SDGs 6, 12-15: water, climate, life on land and sea"
  Prosperity -> "SDGs 7-11: energy, work, industry, cities"
  Partnership -> "SDG 17: finance, technology, trade cooperation"
@/diagram

[tip]
Memory aid: *People, Planet, Prosperity, Peace, Partnership* —
"two Ps in the middle hold the people and the planet together."
Sample goals to memorise with numbers: SDG 1 no poverty, SDG 4
quality education, SDG 5 gender equality, SDG 13 climate action,
SDG 17 partnerships.
[/tip]

Note that the SDGs are **indivisible and integrated**: you cannot
deliver clean water (SDG 6) without decent sanitation and health
(SDG 3), and climate action (SDG 13) conditions almost everything
else. Trade-offs exist too — cheap coal power raises incomes today
and temperatures tomorrow.

## 7. India's SDG and Climate Architecture

NITI Aayog is the nodal agency for the 2030 Agenda. Its **SDG India
Index** localises the goals to states and districts: the national
score climbed from 57 (2018 baseline) to 66 (2020-21) and 71
(2023-24), with Kerala consistently first and the Aspirational
Districts Programme tracking 112 of the most laggard districts
monthly.

India's climate commitments, made under the Paris Agreement (2015)
and updated in 2022 — its Nationally Determined Contributions:

- Cut the **emissions intensity of GDP by 45 per cent by 2030**
  from 2005 levels.
- Achieve about **50 per cent of installed electric power capacity
  from non-fossil sources by 2030**.
- Create an additional **carbon sink of 2.5-3 billion tonnes** of
  CO2-equivalent through forests.

At the **COP26 summit in Glasgow (November 2021)**, the Prime
Minister additionally pledged **net-zero emissions by 2070**, with
interim elements including 500 GW of non-fossil capacity by 2030.
Installed non-fossil capacity has since crossed about 200 GW,
placing India among the world's largest renewable-energy markets.

## 8. How the Two Frameworks Meet

Human development and sustainable development are not rival
syllabus topics; they are two lenses on one question — *development
of what, for whom, and until when?*

- SDG 3 (health), SDG 4 (education), SDG 6 (water), and SDG 7
  (energy) are literally the HDI's dimensions plus access inputs.
- Both frameworks reject income-centrism: one replaces it with
  capabilities, the other with intergenerational responsibility.
- Both demand **distribution-sensitive** data — hence IHDI for the
  HDI and "leave no one behind" as the SDG slogan.
- India's development story ties them together: poverty exit at
  record speed (human development) achieved partly by electrifying
  villages and expanding cooking-gas connections — gains that must
  now be reconciled with decarbonisation (sustainability).

[info]
A useful exam lens: **HDI = people now; sustainability = people
later; the SDGs = the bridge**, with SDG 1-8 repairing today's
shortfalls and SDG 12-15 protecting tomorrow's resource base. Green
GDP, environmental accounting, and ESG investing are the measurement
instruments on that bridge.
[/info]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Item | Fact |
| --- | --- |
| HDI dimensions | Health, education, standard of living |
| HDI aggregation | Geometric mean of three sub-indices |
| HDI bands | Low <0.550; medium 0.550-0.699; high 0.700-0.799; very high 0.800+ |
| India's HDI | About 0.64; medium band; rank in low 130s of 193 |
| HDR originators | Mahbub ul Haq with Amartya Sen; first HDR 1990 |
| MPI | 10 indicators; India 55.1% (2005-06) to 16.4% (2019-21) |
| Brundtland definition | 1987; needs of present without robbing the future |
| SDGs | Adopted 2015; 17 goals, 169 targets; effective 2016 |
| Five Ps | People, Planet, Prosperity, Peace, Partnership |
| India NDC | 45% intensity cut and 50% non-fossil capacity by 2030; net zero 2070 |
